    "agent_messages": [
      "I added `POST /purchases/{id}/reopen`. It builds and passes `go vet`, but it has not been run against a database. Private credentials aren't available and the repo has no tests (`go test ./...` only reports \"no test files\"), so none of the success, replay, 503 or concurrency behaviour has been exercised.\n\nThe code is in a new file, `internal/candidate/reopen.go`, and follows the same pattern as `reject.go`. In `approval.go`, the existing bulk-approve/bulk-reject handler is now the fallback behind `protocol.Item(\"reopen\", \"requester\", ...)`. Other paths still reach the existing handlers unchanged.\n\nHow a request is handled, all in one transaction:\n- **Setup:** it sets the guarded RLS context from the authenticated identity and takes a lock on (`reopen.v5`, tenant, user, key). That lock is why two requests with the same key and same body give two 200s but only one commit.\n- **Replay:** if an `ActionRequest` row already exists for that key, a matching hash returns the stored response with `Idempotency-Replayed: true`. A different purchase returns 409.\n- **The change:** `UPDATE ... SET status='submitted'`, limited to purchases the caller created in this tenant that are `rejected`. With two different keys on the same purchase, the second request waits on the row lock, then matches nothing and gets 409.\n- **Ineligible targets:** if no row was updated, it returns 404 when the purchase doesn't exist in this tenant, otherwise 409. Nothing is written.\n- **Writes on success:** an `ApprovalAudit` row (`rejected`→`submitted`, with the reason), a `purchase.reopened` `PurchaseEvent`, and the `ActionRequest` row (`reopen.v5`, response `{\"id\":...,\"status\":\"submitted\"}`), then commit. If any write fails, everything rolls back and the adapter returns 503, so the same key can be retried. The audit row keeps the nightly status-vs-audit drift check from flagging reopened purchases, and the event is what the notification job already expects.\n\nDecision for you: a replay with the same key and purchase but a different reason returns the original 200 instead of 409. Two things stop the code from comparing reasons:\n- The stored request hash can't include the reason. The nightly reconciliation job recomputes it from the stored response alone, so any other hash would flag every reopen row.\n- The schema only lets approvers read `ApprovalAudit`, where the reason is stored.\n\nRejecting a changed reason with 409 would need a schema or policy change, which this task doesn't allow.\n\n`gofmt` flags `internal/protocol/protocol.go`. That was already the case and the file is protected, so I left it alone."
    ],
